g, factorial design, NVE metric definition, environments\n- Section 4: Experiments (13 subsections covering cross-env trigger, causal control, ablations, dual pathways, RSSM fidelity, entropy analysis, value bias, embedding analysis, threshold sensitivity, gradient surgery, truncation pilot)\n- Section 5: Discussion — CIVO mechanism, MPC vs imagination, multiple pathways, practical implications\n- Section 6: Conclusion\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, brief)\n- **Temporal abstraction in RL**: Options framework [2], FiGAR [7], action repeat [8]\n- **World-model RL**: DreamerV3 [1]\n- **Variable-rate planning in world models**: TAWM [4] — MPC-based, no persistent critic\n- **Value overestimation in MBRL**: Model exploitation [6], delusional bias [5]\n- References: 10 total ([1]-[10])\n\n### Paper Statistics\n- 11 pages (8 main + references + tables)\n- 19 tables, 0 figures\n- 10 references\n- Single-environment gradient surgery decomposition (Walker Walk only)\n- 3 DMC environments, ~30 experiment configurations\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n- Is CIVO genuinely novel? How does it relate to known value overestimation phenomena in MBRL (Janner et al. 2019, Lu et al. 2018)?\n- The paper claims CIVO is \"architecture-specific\" (imagination-based AC but not MPC) — is this distinction novel and significant?\n- The dual-pathway discovery — is this a genuine insight or a natural consequence of the experimental setup?\n- NVE as a diagnostic metric — novel contribution or straightforward ratio?\n- The \"first systematic causal analysis\" claim — is it justified?\n- Gradient decomposition finding (93% obs decoders) — how surprising/novel is this?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n- Is the related work section adequate? Only 10 references is very thin for a top venue.\n- **Missing related work** — consider:\n  - Dreamer variants (DreamerV2, DreamerPro, etc.)\n  - Other world-model architectures (IRIS, TransDreamer, etc.)\n  - Value overestimation beyond MBRL (TD3, SAC clipped double-Q, etc.)\n  - Temporal difference learning at multiple timescales (TD(λ), multi-step returns)\n  - Hierarchical RL beyond options (HAM, MAXQ, goal-conditioned HRL)\n  - Model error and compounding in MBRL (Talvitie 2014, Asadi et al. 2019, etc.)\n  - Action repetition / frame skip literature more broadly\n  - THICK, Hieros (mentioned in discussion but not cited)\n- How well does the paper position itself relative to existing value overestimation literature?\n- Is the MPC vs imagination-based AC distinction well-grounded in prior work?\n\n### Significance and Impact\n- Who is the target audience? How large is this audience?\n- Is this a \"diagnostic analysis\" or does it offer actionable solutions?\n- Would this change how people build temporal abstraction in world models?\n- Is the scope (DreamerV3-specific) too narrow or appropriately focused?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, at least 5]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, at least 5]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be discussed]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Formatting, clarity, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: [1-4]\n- Related Work Coverage: [1-4]\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-6]\n- Confidence: [1-5]\n```\n","uuid":"b8db455c-bd76-48d4-beaa-1dde64813fa6","session_id":"a884cd18-9ddc-4e56-828b-637c4a5e9c11"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\nThe paper diagnoses why temporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3 (imagination-based actor-critic) but succeeds in MPC frameworks like TAWM. The root cause: **CIVO (Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation)** — trained dt-embedding learns observation-specific temporal patterns (93% gradient from observation decoders), creating systematic reward prediction bias at dt>1 states, driving a positive feedback loop in the critic.\n\n### Key Claims\n1. Trained dt-embedding is the necessary and sufficient trigger for CIVO, validated across 3 DMC environments with 18/18 NVE threshold classification\n2. NVE 1.5x threshold achieves perfect binary classification (AUROC=1.0) as a simple diagnostic\n3. Dual vulnerability pathways: Pathway A (classic CIVO, value-driven, entropy↓) and Pathway B (policy instability, eval-driven, entropy↑) — explains why dt=1 imagination doesn't fix it\n4. Full Stop-Gradient Surgery quantifies CIVO's independent contribution at 35pp beyond inherent multi-dt cost\n5. Establishes MPC/imagination architectural divergence as the key explanatory factor (MPC evaluates at inference time without persistent critic training)\n\n### Structure\n- Section 1: Introduction — motivates the MPC vs imagination divergence question\n- Section 2: Background — options framework, DreamerV3, TAWM, value overestimation in MBRL\n- Section 3: Method — architecture with dt-embedding, factorial design, NVE metric definition, environments\n- Section 4: Experiments (13 subsections covering cross-env trigger, causal control, ablations, dual pathways, RSSM fidelity, entropy analysis, value bias, embedding analysis, threshold sensitivity, gradient surgery, truncation pilot)\n- Section 5: Discussion — CIVO mechanism, MPC vs imagination, multiple pathways, practical implications\n- Section 6: Conclusion\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, brief)\n- **Temporal abstraction in RL**: Options framework [2], FiGAR [7], action repeat [8]\n- **World-model RL**: DreamerV3 [1]\n- **Variable-rate planning in world models**: TAWM [4] — MPC-based, no persistent critic\n- **Value overestimation in MBRL**: Model exploitation [6], delusional bias [5]\n- References: 10 total ([1]-[10])\n\n### Paper Statistics\n- 11 pages (8 main + references + tables)\n- 19 tables, 0 figures\n- 10 references\n- Single-environment gradient surgery decomposition (Walker Walk only)\n- 3 DMC environments, ~30 experiment configurations\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n- Is CIVO genuinely novel? How does it relate to known value overestimation phenomena in MBRL (Janner et al. 2019, Lu et al. 2018)?\n- The paper claims CIVO is \"architecture-specific\" (imagination-based AC but not MPC) — is this distinction novel and significant?\n- The dual-pathway discovery — is this a genuine insight or a natural consequence of the experimental setup?\n- NVE as a diagnostic metric — novel contribution or straightforward ratio?\n- The \"first systematic causal analysis\" claim — is it justified?\n- Gradient decomposition finding (93% obs decoders) — how surprising/novel is this?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n- Is the related work section adequate? Only 10 references is very thin for a top venue.\n- **Missing related work** — consider:\n  - Dreamer variants (DreamerV2, DreamerPro, etc.)\n  - Other world-model architectures (IRIS, TransDreamer, etc.)\n  - Value overestimation beyond MBRL (TD3, SAC clipped double-Q, etc.)\n  - Temporal difference learning at multiple timescales (TD(λ), multi-step returns)\n  - Hierarchical RL beyond options (HAM, MAXQ, goal-conditioned HRL)\n  - Model error and compounding in MBRL (Talvitie 2014, Asadi et al. 2019, etc.)\n  - Action repetition / frame skip literature more broadly\n  - THICK, Hieros (mentioned in discussion but not cited)\n- How well does the paper position itself relative to existing value overestimation literature?\n- Is the MPC vs imagination-based AC distinction well-grounded in prior work?\n\n### Significance and Impact\n- Who is the target audience? How large is this audience?\n- Is this a \"diagnostic analysis\" or does it offer actionable solutions?\n- Would this change how people build temporal abstraction in world models?\n- Is the scope (DreamerV3-specific) too narrow or appropriately focused?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, at least 5]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, at least 5]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be discussed]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed paragraph]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Formatting, clarity, etc.]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: [1-4]\n- Related Work Coverage: [1-4]\n- Significance: [1-4]\n- Overall: [1-6]\n- Confidence: [1-5]\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DrqMuURWtByLk8Lt6EW9k5","session_id":"a884cd18-9ddc-4e56-828b-637c4a5e9c11","uuid":"05702fe7-028b-4d7c-8938-bf03b74244ff","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:56:33.752Z"}
