{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WNrrA1GdfDfFNhRjZKAc4e","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/report.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Handover — 2026-05-16\\\\n\\\\n> 由 Agent 生成，每次状态变更后自动更新。硬限 150 行。\\\\n\\\\n## 运行中\\\\n| exp_id | 假设 | 状态 | 关键指标 | W&B |\\\\n|--------|------|------|---------|-----|\\\\n| exp_008f_s3_no_dt_embed_walker | Walker Walk dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON + H=8 seed 3 — 008f 系列第 3 个独立 seed，闭合 no_dt_emb condition 的 bootstrap CI（当前 n=2，需 ≥3）。预期 NVE<1.5×，无 CIVO。 | — | — | — |\\\\n| exp_020_vanilla_dreamerv3_s2 | Vanilla DreamerV3 (no dt_emb, no subsample, no gate) seed 2 — Pathway B 控制实验：验证 008e_v3_s2 的 eval-driven collapse 是否需要 trained dt_emb 作为必要条件。如果 vanilla s2 稳定 → C6 成立 (trained dt_emb 是 Pathway B 必要条件)。如果也崩溃 → Pathway B 是 DreamerV3 inherent fragility。 | — | — | — |\\\\n\\\\n## 待处理\\\\n- exp_019b_pickcube_civo_trigger: PickCube-v1 CIVO trigger — trained dt_emb, subsample ON, dt=2 imagination, H=8. 预期触发 CIVO（trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x → collapse），验证 CIVO 在 manipulation 环境是否成立（排队等待 GPU）\\\\n\\\\n## 近期结论\\\\n- **exp_018v2_full_sgs_walker_s1** (success): Full-SGS 修正版: trained dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 + H=8 + full_sgs=True (detach all non-KL gradients from dt_emb). 修复 018 配置错误（缺失 gate 参数）。如果 CIVO 被消除 (NVE<1.5×) → 确认 obs decoder gradient 是 CIVO root cause → C5 成立。 ← 基于 exp_016e_sgs_walker_s1\\\\n  [018v2 CIVO Decomposition Analysis](/api/workspace-files/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/exp_018v2_full_sgs_walker_s1/data/018v2_civo_analysis.json)\\\\n- **exp_010b_cheetah_civo_trigger** (success): Cheetah Run CIVO trigger — trained dt_emb, subsample ON, dt=2, H=8, seed 1. 跨环境验证：Walker 上 008d 配置在 Cheetah 上是否同样触发 CIVO (NVE>1.5x)。若成立→回应 reviewer \\\\\\\"single environment\\\\\\\" concern。 ← 基于 exp_010a_cheetah_baseline_s1\\\\n- **exp_019a_pickcube_baseline** (killed): PickCube-v1 baseline — dt=1, no dt_emb (dim=0), subsample OFF. ManiSkill3 manipulation 环境稳定基线，验证 CIVO 诊断在 manipulation 中的适用性\\\\n\\\\n## 趋势\\\\n成功 11 / 负向 13 / 失败 6 / 共 44\\\\neval_return_peak: 499.58 ↓（近 3 次 success）\\\\neval_return_peak_step: 75000 ↓（近 3 次 success）\\\\n\\\\n## 下一步\\\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\\\n\\\\n## 资源状态\\\\nbjb1-45299: 无 GPU 信息\\\\n\\\\n## 告警\\\\n- exp_002_event_gated: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- exp_006: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_008a: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_008c: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_008d: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_008e_subsample_on_fixed_dt1: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_008e_subsample_on_dt1_h15: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_008e_v2_subsample_on_dt1_h15: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_009d_baseline_walker_s1: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_009f_008f_walker_s1: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_017_subsample_off_trained_dt_emb: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_016_sgs_walker_s1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- exp_009g_008f_walker_s0: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_009h_008f_walker_s0: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- exp_016b_sgs_walker_s1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- exp_016c_sgs_walker_s1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- exp_016d_sgs_walker_s1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- 016e: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_018_full_sgs_walker_s1: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- exp_019a_pickcube_baseline: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 2732, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DrqMuURWtByLk8Lt6EW9k5","session_id":"a884cd18-9ddc-4e56-828b-637c4a5e9c11","uuid":"2dd7af03-ffc6-44e3-97d8-7fe77837f287","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:56:39.918Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0154HtJKyoKNS1FXAeBXe8h6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T062606\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T07:15:12.795193+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## R1 Review: Methodology and Experiments\n9\t\n10\t### Summary\n11\t\n12\tThis paper presents a factorial diagnostic investigation of why variable-rate imagination (dt>1) fails in DreamerV3's RSSM-based world model. The core finding is that trained duration embeddings enable the critic to form dt-specific value estimates grounded in inaccurate multi-step predictions, producing systematic value overestimation (\"delusional critic\") and eventual performance collapse. The paper introduces a normalized value/eval overshoot ratio (R_norm) as a diagnostic and claims 9/9 collapse under trained dt-embedding vs. 9/9 stability without it, across 3 DMC environments and 3 seeds each.\n13\t\n14\t### Strengths\n15\t\n16\t- S1: **Exemplary controlled experiment design (008d vs 008f).** The single-variable isolation between trained dt-embedding (008d) and removed dt-embedding (008f), with all other factors held constant (subsample ON, H=8, dt=2), is a textbook-quality controlled experiment. The binary outcome (collapse vs. stability) with the massive effect size on R_norm (1.71x vs 0.73x at 35K) leaves little ambiguity about the causal role of dt-conditioning. This is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper.\n17\t\n18\t- S2: **Multi-level diagnostic methodology.** The paper goes beyond showing \"it fails\" to tracing a complete causal chain through multiple independently verifiable links: RSSM fidelity gap (Section 4.1) -> embedding differentiation (cosine similarity analysis, Section 4.6) -> value overestimation (R_norm, distributional analysis) -> actor entropy rise (Section 4.7) -> performance collapse. Each link is supported by distinct metrics, making the argument robust to any single metric being questioned.\n19\t\n20\t- S3: **Well-defined diagnostic metric.** The normalized v/e overshoot ratio R_norm(t) = [V(t)/E(t)] / [V_base(t)/E_base(t)] is a principled normalization that accounts for the natural increase in value/eval ratio during healthy training. This is superior to raw value/eval ratio and addresses the confound that baseline v/e reaches ~70% asymptotically. The metric is clearly defined and reproducible.\n21\t\n22\t- S4: **Confound elimination is systematic.** The paper progressively eliminates alternative hypotheses: compounding error (refuted by 008c H=8 underperforming 008a H=15), subsample data composition (refuted by 008e_v2 untrained + subsample ON showing no collapse), and horizon length alone (008c vs 008a). The logic of elimination is well-structured even if some comparisons involve confounded changes.\n23\t\n24\t- S5: **The untrained-embedding value suppression finding (C3) is genuinely interesting.** The observation that frozen random embeddings structurally prevent optimistic drift, producing V-shaped recovery cycles at 84% of baseline over 105K steps, is a non-obvious finding with potential implications for how auxiliary conditioning interacts with model-based value estimation more broadly.\n25\t\n26\t### Weaknesses\n27\t\n28\t- W1: **[CRITICAL] Substantial fraction of reported results appear to be projections, not empirical data.** Cross-referencing the paper's claims against the project's experiment registry and memory reveals a severe data provenance problem. The project memory (as of 2026-05-14) shows the following experiments are actually completed with real data: Walker Walk baseline (seed 0), 008a (seed 0, 112K steps), 008c (seed 0), 008d (seed 0, killed at 45K), 008f (seed 0, at ~35K), and 008e_v3 (seed 0, at ~45K). The multi-seed Walker Walk data (seeds 1-2 for baseline, 008d, 008f, 008a), ALL Cheetah Run data, and ALL Hopper Hop data originate from the \"dream simulation\" (imagined_experiments.yaml), not from actual training runs. The paper's headline claim of \"9/9 collapse, 9/9 stable across 3 environments x 3 seeds\" is therefore largely built on simulated/projected numbers. The paper does not distinguish these. This is a fatal methodological flaw if not corrected. The actual empirical evidence supports at best \"1/1 collapse, 1/1 stable in Walker Walk single-seed\" for the core 008d-vs-008f comparison, plus supporting single-seed evidence from other Walker Walk configurations.\n29\t\n30\t- W2: **[MAJOR] The RSSM fidelity measurement (Table 1) lacks critical methodological detail.** The paper reports observation reconstruction loss of 0.42 (dt=1) vs 1.15 (dt=2), yielding a 2.74x ratio. However: (a) It is unclear when during training these measurements are taken — from a converged world model or during training? (b) Are these measured on the RSSM's own training data (in-distribution) or on held-out transitions? (c) For the dt=2 case, is the RSSM trained with subsample ON (so it has seen dt=2 transitions) or subsample OFF (so dt=2 is out-of-distribution)? This matters enormously: a 2.74x gap on data the model was trained on is very different from a 2.74x gap on out-of-distribution data. (d) The prompt summary gives different numbers (0.025/0.089 for obs, 0.008/0.041 for reward) than the paper (0.42/1.15 and 0.08/0.31), suggesting these measurements may not be stable or well-defined. The fidelity gap is the stated \"physical foundation\" of the entire causal chain, so it requires rigorous measurement methodology.\n31\t\n32\t- W3: **[MAJOR] The 1.5x R_norm threshold is post-hoc and validated on the same data it was derived from.** The paper claims R_norm > 1.5x \"reliably precedes collapse\" and perfectly separates all 18 runs with \"zero misclassification.\" But (a) the threshold was clearly derived by observing which values separated collapse from non-collapse in the existing data, (b) it has never been validated on held-out configurations or environments, and (c) the claim of \"environment-agnostic\" reliability rests on projected data from environments that have not been run. Even restricting to real data, the threshold was calibrated on Walker Walk and has exactly one environment's data to evaluate it on. Presenting a post-hoc threshold as a validated diagnostic tool without any out-of-sample validation is methodologically problematic. The paper should at minimum report the actual separation margin (what is the highest R_norm of any stable run and the lowest R_norm of any collapsed run?) and discuss whether 1.5x is a sharp phase transition or a post-hoc cut.\n33\t\n34\t- W4: **[MAJOR] Incomplete factorial grid with confounded comparisons.** The paper presents a factorial design crossing dt_embedding (trained/untrained/removed) x subsample (ON/OFF) x horizon (8/15), but the grid is sparse. The \"critical\" 008d-vs-008f comparison is clean (single variable), but many other comparisons change multiple variables simultaneously. For example, 008a (untrained, subsample OFF, H=15) vs 008d (trained, subsample ON, H=8) differs in ALL three factors. The paper acknowledges this partially with 008e_v2 (untrained, subsample ON, H=15), but the full 2x3x2 grid would require 12 cells, and only 5 are filled (some with projected data). Missing cells include: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 (would this also collapse? if so, it strengthens C2; if not, it complicates the story), and removed dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 (what performance does pure dt=2 imagination achieve without any embedding at all?). The incomplete grid leaves alternative explanations partially open.\n35\t\n36\t- W5: **[MODERATE] n=3 seeds is insufficient for quantitative claims about thresholds and ratios, though adequate for the binary collapse/no-collapse finding.** For the qualitative claim (trained dt-emb causes collapse, removed dt-emb does not), the binary 3/3 vs 0/3 outcome has p=0.05 by Fisher's exact test (one-tailed) — barely significant and only if the data is real (see W1). For quantitative claims like \"R_norm = 2.06 +/- 0.13 at 45K\" or \"Cohen's d > 10,\" three seeds provide very unstable estimates of variance. The confidence interval on std from n=3 is enormous. The paper should not report Cohen's d from n=3 as if it is a reliable effect size estimate. For the multi-environment claim, each environment has only 3 seeds per condition — and again, only Walker Walk seed 0 appears to be real data.\n37\t\n38\t- W6: **[MODERATE] The causal chain has a logical gap between \"RSSM fidelity gap exists\" and \"trained dt-embedding enables exploitation.\"** The paper shows that RSSM prediction quality degrades at dt=2 (link 1), and that trained dt-embedding causes collapse while removed dt-embedding does not (link 2 -> link 4). But the mechanistic connection — HOW the critic exploits the fidelity gap through dt-conditioning — is argued by narrative, not measured directly. The distributional analysis (Table 7, Section 4.8) shows right-skewed overestimation, but does not directly demonstrate that overestimation is concentrated on dt=2 imagination steps specifically (vs dt=1 steps in the same trajectory). A direct comparison of V(s, dt=1) vs V(s, dt=2) prediction errors would close this gap. The cosine similarity analysis shows temporal coupling but not causation — the embeddings could differentiate for benign reasons that happen to coincide with collapse.\n39\t\n40\t- W7: **[MODERATE] All environments are DMC locomotion tasks with dense rewards and continuous control.** Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and Hopper Hop share similar state representations (joint angles/velocities), action spaces (continuous torque), reward structures (dense, smooth), and episode lengths. Testing on visually diverse but structurally similar tasks does not constitute environment diversity. The claim \"the mechanism generalizes across DMC locomotion tasks\" would be accurate; \"environment-agnostic\" is not. Tasks with sparse rewards, discrete actions, visual observations, or long horizons might exhibit different failure modes or no failure at all.\n41\t\n42\t### Questions for Authors\n43\t\n44\t- Q1: Which of the 18 reported runs (9 collapse, 9 stable) are from completed training runs with actual GPU compute, and which are from the dream simulation / projections? Please provide a complete provenance table.\n45\t\n46\t- Q2: The RSSM fidelity gap numbers differ between the paper summary (obs: 0.025/0.089, reward: 0.008/0.041) and the paper body (obs: 0.42/1.15, reward: 0.08/0.31). Are these different metrics? If so, which one is the correct measurement and what exactly does it measure?\n47\t\n48\t- Q3: For the fidelity measurement (Table 1): was the RSSM trained with subsample ON (exposed to dt=2 transitions) or OFF? At what training step were measurements taken? On what data (training set, held-out, or imagination rollouts)?\n49\t\n50\t- Q4: In the 008d-vs-008f comparison, 008f removes the dt-embedding entirely (dim=0). This means the critic receives strictly less information. Is it possible that the \"protection\" is simply due to reduced model capacity / fewer parameters, rather than specifically blocking dt-conditioned exploitation? Have you compared against a control where the embedding exists but is a constant (e.g., always the dt=1 embedding regardless of actual dt)?\n51\t\n52\t- Q5: For the value distribution analysis (Table 7): is the overestimation specifically on dt=2 imagination steps, or is it diffuse across all imagination steps? Can you decompose V predictions by the dt of the imagination step that generated them?\n53\t\n54\t- Q6: The 008f configuration achieves only ~230 eval return (24% of baseline 950). While it avoids collapse, this is a catastrophic performance level. How should practitioners interpret this? Is the recommendation to avoid dt-embedding equivalent to recommending against dt>1 imagination entirely (since without dt-embedding, performance is 24% of baseline)?\n55\t\n56\t- Q7: How sensitive is the collapse finding to the dt-embedding dimension? You tested dim=32 (collapse) and dim=0 (no collapse). What about dim=1, dim=4, dim=16? Is there a smooth transition or a sharp threshold?\n57\t\n58\t### Missing Experiments / Analyses\n59\t\n60\t- **M1: Complete the claimed multi-seed and multi-environment experiments with real training runs.** This is the single most important action item. The paper's central empirical claim cannot rest on projected data.\n61\t\n62\t- **M2: Fill the missing factorial cells.** Most critically: trained dt-emb + subsample OFF + H=15 (to isolate whether trained dt-emb causes collapse even without subsample ON training data), and removed dt-emb + subsample OFF + H=15 (to establish the upper bound of dt=2 performance without any embedding).\n63\t\n64\t- **M3: Direct measurement of per-dt-step value prediction errors.** Decompose value predictions in imagination by the dt of each step. Show that overestimation is concentrated on dt=2 steps specifically, not uniformly distributed.\n65\t\n66\t- **M4: Sensitivity analysis of the 1.5x threshold.** Report the actual separation gap. Compute ROC-style analysis across threshold values. Discuss whether this is a phase transition or a continuous degradation that happens to be captured by 1.5x on this data.\n67\t\n68\t- **M5: At least one non-locomotion environment** (e.g., Cartpole Swingup, manipulation task, or a sparse-reward variant) to test generalization beyond the current narrow task family.\n69\t\n70\t- **M6: dt-embedding dimension sweep** (dim=1, 4, 8, 16, 32) to characterize whether collapse onset depends on embedding capacity.\n71\t\n72\t- **M7: A mitigation pilot experiment.** Even one experiment showing that the diagnosis enables a fix (e.g., conservative value clipping when R_norm > threshold, ensemble critics, or MOPO-style uncertainty penalty) would substantially increase the paper's impact and actionability.\n73\t\n74\t- **M8: Formal statistical testing.** Report p-values from Fisher's exact test for the binary collapse/no-collapse comparison. Report bootstrap confidence intervals for R_norm differences rather than simple mean +/- std from n=3.\n75\t\n76\t### Minor Issues\n77\t\n78\t- The term \"delusional critic\" should be more carefully distinguished from the formal definition of delusional policies in Lu et al. (2018, NeurIPS). The overestimation here arises from model inaccuracy combined with conditioning, not from function approximation class constraints. The distinction matters for readers familiar with the formal notion.\n79\t\n80\t- Table 10 in the summary shows Walker Walk trained best = 485 (51%) and no dt-emb best = 207 (22%). But 008a (untrained dt-emb) achieves 778 (82%). The \"best\" column should clarify which configuration it refers to, as \"trained\" and \"untrained\" are distinct from \"removed.\"\n81\t\n82\t- The paper claims the 008f eval \"rising\" at 35K (197 -> 207), but with only 2 data points and no error bars, this trend is not statistically meaningful. Avoid causal language (\"removing dt-embedding improves performance\") from such sparse data.\n83\t\n84\t- The actor entropy analysis (Table 8) shows values at 10K intervals. Given that collapse occurs over ~20K steps (25K-45K), higher temporal resolution would strengthen the claim that entropy rise \"precedes\" collapse. Currently, the resolution is too coarse to establish temporal ordering.\n85\t\n86\t- Section 3.2 defines dt-embedding as \"concatenated to the RSSM state before input to the critic.\" Clarify whether it is also input to the actor, the RSSM transition model, or only the critic. This matters for interpreting which component exploits the embedding.\n87\t\n88\t### Score: 3/6\n89\t### Confidence: 4/5\n90\t\n91\t**Justification:** The core experimental design (008d vs 008f) is methodologically sound and the mechanistic insight about dt-conditioned exploitation is genuinely novel and non-obvious. However, the critical weakness is data provenance: the paper's headline empirical claims (9/9, 9/9 across 3 environments) appear to rest substantially on projected rather than empirical data, which is a fundamental methodological flaw for an empirical investigation. The RSSM fidelity measurement lacks sufficient detail for reproducibility. The 1.5x threshold has not been validated out-of-sample. If all claimed experiments were actually completed and the numbers held, and if the methodological gaps (fidelity measurement details, threshold validation, factorial grid completion) were addressed, this could be a solid 4-5/6 diagnostic contribution. As submitted, the gap between claimed and verified evidence is too large for acceptance at a 25% acceptance-rate venue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VVjg8XcxFS9zdzgBQKsc21","session_id":"a884cd18-9ddc-4e56-828b-637c4a5e9c11","uuid":"ba2556f8-bfbe-459d-999a-e3ef29c6e39c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:56:39.792Z"}
