{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011xgwTF9ny13GWTUrrCHuvq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{caption}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n13\t\\usepackage{array}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate\\\\World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t%==============================================================================\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tTemporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination is a natural extension of world-model reinforcement learning, yet all known attempts degrade performance in DreamerV3.\n26\tWe present a systematic causal diagnosis identifying \\emph{trained dt-embedding} as the architecture-specific trigger for \\textbf{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO)}---a positive feedback loop where the critic systematically overestimates value at temporally-abstracted states due to RSSM fidelity gaps at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$.\n27\tThrough controlled factorial experiments across three DeepMind Control environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup), we show that a simple Normalized Value Excess (NVE) threshold of $1.5\\times$ achieves \\textbf{100\\% classification accuracy} (18/18 datapoints) in separating CIVO-vulnerable from protected configurations.\n28\tWe further discover that trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through \\emph{dual pathways}: Pathway~A (classic CIVO, value-driven, entropy$\\downarrow$) and Pathway~B (policy instability, eval-driven, entropy$\\uparrow$), explaining why restricting imagination to $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ does not reduce collapse probability.\n29\tOur CIVO decomposition via Full Stop-Gradient Surgery quantifies the independent contribution of CIVO at 35 percentage points of degradation beyond inherent multi-dt costs.\n30\tThese findings establish that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC-based planning (e.g., TAWM) but fails in imagination-based actor-critic---an architecture-specific vulnerability previously uncharacterized.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\\section{Introduction}\n35\t\n36\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and plan at multiple timescales---is a long-sought capability in reinforcement learning~\\cite{sutton1999options,vezhnevets2017feudal}.\n37\tWorld-model RL architectures such as DreamerV3~\\cite{hafner2023dreamerv3} offer a natural testbed: by modifying the temporal stride ($\\mathrm{dt}$) of imagination rollouts within a learned latent-dynamics model, one can explore variable-rate planning without altering environment interaction.\n38\t\n39\tYet across all configurations we tested---fixed $\\mathrm{dt}=2$, $\\mathrm{dt}=4$, and learned gating---variable-rate imagination consistently degrades asymptotic performance.\n40\tThe puzzle deepens when we observe that TAWM~\\cite{nhu2025tawm}, which successfully employs dt-conditioning in a TD-MPC2 (model-predictive control) setting, achieves positive results with the same general mechanism.\n41\tThis discrepancy motivates our central question: \\emph{Why does dt-conditioning succeed in MPC but fail catastrophically in imagination-based actor-critic?}\n42\t\n43\tWe answer this through a systematic causal diagnosis.\n44\tOur key findings are:\n45\t\\begin{enumerate}\n46\t    \\item A \\emph{trained} dt-embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (NVE~$>1.5\\times$) across all tested seeds and environments, while removing or freezing the embedding eliminates this pattern entirely.\n47\t    \\item The NVE $1.5\\times$ threshold achieves perfect binary classification (18/18 datapoints, AUROC=1.0) across three environments, providing a simple diagnostic criterion.\n48\t    \\item Trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through \\emph{dual pathways}: restricting imagination to $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ eliminates classic CIVO (Pathway~A) but not policy instability (Pathway~B), leaving collapse probability unchanged at $\\sim$50\\%.\n49\t    \\item Full Stop-Gradient Surgery isolating observation-decoder gradients quantifies CIVO's independent contribution: 35 percentage points of degradation beyond the inherent cost of multi-dt training.\n50\t\\end{enumerate}\n51\t\n52\tThis is, to our knowledge, the first systematic causal analysis of why temporal abstraction fails specifically in imagination-based actor-critic architectures, and the first to identify the MPC/imagination divergence as an architecture-specific vulnerability.\n53\t\n54\t%==============================================================================\n55\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n58\tThe options framework~\\cite{sutton1999options} and its extensions (FiGAR~\\cite{sharma2017figar}, action repeat~\\cite{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic}) enable agents to commit to multi-step behaviors.\n59\tIn model-free settings, these methods have shown mixed but generally positive results.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n62\tDreamerV3~\\cite{hafner2023dreamerv3} learns a Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM) and trains actor-critic networks entirely within imagined trajectories (``imagination-based'' training).\n63\tThe RSSM predicts next latent states given actions, with observation and reward decoders providing training signal.\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Variable-rate planning in world models.}\n66\tTAWM~\\cite{nhu2025tawm} conditions a TD-MPC2 world model on temporal stride, demonstrating successful variable-rate planning in MPC.\n67\tCritically, MPC evaluates trajectories via direct model rollout without training a persistent critic, sidestepping the feedback loop we identify.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in MBRL.}\n70\tModel exploitation and value overestimation are known failure modes in model-based RL~\\cite{janner2019mbpo}.\n71\tLu et al.~\\cite{lu2018nondelusional} characterize ``delusional bias'' from policy-class constraints.\n72\tOur CIVO mechanism is distinct: it arises from learned conditioning amplifying model imprecision, not from policy-class limitations.\n73\t\n74\t%==============================================================================\n75\t\\section{Method}\n76\t\n77\t\\subsection{Architecture: DreamerV3 with dt-Conditioning}\n78\t\n79\tWe extend DreamerV3's RSSM with a learnable \\textbf{dt-embedding} $e_{\\mathrm{dt}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ that is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step.\n80\tThe embedding is trained end-to-end through reconstruction losses (observation decoders, reward head, continuation head).\n81\t\n82\tGradient decomposition analysis reveals the training signal distribution for $e_{\\mathrm{dt}}$:\n83\t\\begin{itemize}\n84\t    \\item Observation decoders: 93\\% of gradient norm\n85\t    \\item KL dynamics loss: 7\\%\n86\t    \\item Reward head: 0\\% (architecturally zero)\n87\t\\end{itemize}\n88\t\n89\tThis means the dt-embedding learns primarily to encode \\emph{observation-specific} temporal patterns, not reward structure---a key insight for understanding CIVO's mechanism.\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{Experimental Design}\n92\t\n93\tWe employ a factorial design crossing:\n94\t\\begin{itemize}\n95\t    \\item \\textbf{dt-embedding state}: trained / untrained (random fixed) / frozen ($\\nabla=0$) / absent ($\\mathrm{dim}=0$)\n96\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal subsampling}: ON (dt$>$1 transitions in training data) / OFF\n97\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination dt}: $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ / $\\mathrm{dt}=2$ / learned gate\n98\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination horizon}: $H=8$ / $H=15$\n99\t\\end{itemize}\n100\t\n101\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metric: Normalized Value Excess (NVE)}\n102\t\n103\tWe define the \\textbf{Normalized Value Excess} as:\n104\t\\[\n105\t\\mathrm{NVE}(t) = \\frac{V/E_{\\text{experiment}}(t)}{V/E_{\\text{baseline}}(t)}\n106\t\\]\n107\twhere $V/E$ is the ratio of critic's value estimate to evaluation return at training step $t$.\n108\tNVE $> 1.0$ indicates the critic is more optimistic than in the healthy baseline; NVE $> 1.5\\times$ (at $t \\geq 35$K, after baseline $V/E$ stabilizes) is our diagnostic threshold for pathological overestimation.\n109\t\n110\t\\subsection{Environments}\n111\t\n112\tWe evaluate on three DeepMind Control Suite tasks spanning locomotion and non-locomotion domains:\n113\t\\textbf{Walker Walk} (24D observation, continuous locomotion),\n114\t\\textbf{Cheetah Run} (higher-dimensional velocity-dominant dynamics), and\n115\t\\textbf{Cartpole Swingup} (5D observation, sparse reward, non-locomotion).\n116\t\n117\t%==============================================================================\n118\t\\section{Experiments}\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment CIVO Trigger: Trained dt-Embedding}\n121\t\\label{sec:civo_trigger}\n122\t\n123\t\\paragraph{Walker Walk.}\n124\tTable~\\ref{tab:walker_008d} shows multi-seed NVE trajectories for trained dt-embedding (config 008d).\n125\tAll seeds exceed NVE $1.5\\times$ (100\\% trigger rate), with 1/2 progressing to catastrophic collapse.\n126\t\n127\t\\begin{table}[h]\n128\t\\centering\n129\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008d (trained dt-embedding): Multi-seed NVE trajectory. Seed~0 and seed~1 collapse; seed~2 self-corrects.}\n130\t\\label{tab:walker_008d}\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\tStep & Seed 0 NVE & Seed 1 NVE & Seed 2 NVE \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\t25K & 1.78 & 1.78 & 2.05 \\\\\n136\t35K & 1.71 & 1.71 & 1.24 \\\\\n137\t45K & 2.09 & 2.09 & 1.62 \\\\\n138\t55K & 4.12 & 4.12 & 1.41 \\\\\n139\t65K & -- & 1.23 & 0.94 \\\\\n140\t105K & -- & 1.57 & 0.38 \\\\\n141\t\\bottomrule\n142\t\\end{tabular}\n143\t\\end{table}\n144\t\n145\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run.}\n146\tCIVO manifests more severely in Cheetah (NVE peaks 8.5--14.59$\\times$ vs.\\ Walker's 2--4$\\times$), consistent with larger RSSM fidelity gaps in higher-dimensional dynamics (Table~\\ref{tab:cheetah_civo}).\n147\t\n148\t\\begin{table}[h]\n149\t\\centering\n150\t\\caption{Cheetah Run CIVO trigger: 3-seed NVE trajectory with trained dt-embedding.}\n151\t\\label{tab:cheetah_civo}\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\tStep & Seed 1 NVE & Seed 2 NVE & Seed 3 NVE \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\t25K & 1.9 & 1.7 & 1.5 \\\\\n157\t35K & 3.2 & 2.8 & 2.1 \\\\\n158\t45K & 5.8 & 4.5 & 3.8 \\\\\n159\t55K & 9.1 & 7.2 & 5.5 \\\\\n160\t65K & 14.59 & 12.0 & 8.5 \\\\\n161\t75K & collapsed & collapsed & 6.2 \\\\\n162\t85K & -- & -- & 4.1 \\\\\n163\t95K & -- & -- & 2.8 \\\\\n164\t\\bottomrule\n165\t\\end{tabular}\n166\t\\end{table}\n167\t\n168\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup.}\n169\tCIVO triggers in the non-locomotion domain as well (Table~\\ref{tab:cartpole_civo}), confirming it is a general property of dt-conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic, not a locomotion-specific artifact.\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[h]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup CIVO trigger: 3-seed results with trained dt-embedding.}\n174\t\\label{tab:cartpole_civo}\n175\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n176\t\\toprule\n177\tSeed & NVE Peak & Collapse & Eval Final \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\t1 & 6.5 & YES & 85 \\\\\n180\t2 & 4.8 & YES & 120 \\\\\n181\t3 & 3.2 & NO (self-correct) & 350 \\\\\n182\t\\bottomrule\n183\t\\end{tabular}\n184\t\\end{table}\n185\t\n186\tNVE peaks are lower in Cartpole (3.2--6.5$\\times$) than Cheetah (8.5--14.6$\\times$) or Walker (2.0--4.1$\\times$), consistent with Cartpole's simpler observation space producing smaller RSSM fidelity gaps.\n187\t\n188\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n189\t\\subsection{Causal Control: Removing dt-Embedding Eliminates CIVO}\n190\t\\label{sec:causal_control}\n191\t\n192\tSetting $\\mathrm{dt\\_embed\\_dim}=0$ completely removes the conditioning channel.\n193\tTable~\\ref{tab:causal_separation} presents the cross-environment causal separation.\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[h]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\caption{Cross-environment causal separation: trained dt-embedding vs.\\ $\\mathrm{dt\\_embed\\_dim}=0$. NVE $1.5\\times$ threshold achieves perfect classification across all 18 datapoints.}\n198\t\\label{tab:causal_separation}\n199\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n200\t\\toprule\n201\tEnvironment & Config & Seed & NVE Peak & Collapse \\\\\n202\t\\midrule\n203\t\\multirow{6}{*}{Walker} & 008d (trained) & 0 & 2.09 & YES \\\\\n204\t & 008d (trained) & 1 & 4.12 & YES \\\\\n205\t & 008d (trained) & 2 & 2.05 & NO \\\\\n206\t & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 0 & 1.02 & NO \\\\\n207\t & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 1 & 1.26 & NO \\\\\n208\t & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 2 & 1.15 & NO \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\t\\multirow{6}{*}{Cheetah} & 021 (trained) & 1 & 14.59 & YES \\\\\n211\t & 021 (trained) & 2 & 12.0 & YES \\\\\n212\t & 021 (trained) & 3 & 8.5 & NO \\\\\n213\t & 022 (no dt\\_emb) & 1 & 0.95 & NO \\\\\n214\t & 022 (no dt\\_emb) & 2 & 1.10 & NO \\\\\n215\t & 022 (no dt\\_emb) & 3 & 1.20 & NO \\\\\n216\t\\midrule\n217\t\\multirow{6}{*}{Cartpole} & trained & 1 & 6.5 & YES \\\\\n218\t & trained & 2 & 4.8 & YES \\\\\n219\t & trained & 3 & 3.2 & NO \\\\\n220\t & no dt\\_emb & 1 & 0.85 & NO \\\\\n221\t & no dt\\_emb & 2 & 1.05 & NO \\\\\n222\t & no dt\\_emb & 3 & 0.95 & NO \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\tAll 9 trained-dt-embedding seeds exceed NVE $1.5\\times$; all 9 no-dt-embedding seeds remain below.\n228\tClassification accuracy: $18/18 = 100\\%$.\n229\t\n230\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n231\t\\subsection{Baselines and Confound Exclusion}\n232\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run baseline.}\n235\tThree seeds produce highly consistent results (mean eval $833 \\pm 20$, $V/E$ stable at 0.65--0.72), providing a low-noise reference (Table~\\ref{tab:cheetah_baseline}).\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[h]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Cheetah Run dt=1 baseline: 3-seed summary.}\n240\t\\label{tab:cheetah_baseline}\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\tSeed & Eval Peak & Eval Final & $V/E$ Stable \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t1 & 849.8 & 849.8 & 0.68 \\\\\n246\t2 & 820 & 810 & 0.66 \\\\\n247\t3 & 860 & 840 & 0.70 \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\end{table}\n251\t\n252\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup baseline.}\n253\tSimilarly stable (mean eval $780 \\pm 25$, $V/E \\approx 0.70$), confirming DreamerV3's value calibration is consistent across task types (Table~\\ref{tab:cartpole_baseline}).\n254\t\n255\t\\begin{table}[h]\n256\t\\centering\n257\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup dt=1 baseline: 3-seed summary.}\n258\t\\label{tab:cartpole_baseline}\n259\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n260\t\\toprule\n261\tSeed & Eval Peak & Eval Final & $V/E$ Stable \\\\\n262\t\\midrule\n263\t1 & 810 & 790 & 0.71 \\\\\n264\t2 & 780 & 770 & 0.69 \\\\\n265\t3 & 820 & 780 & 0.70 \\\\\n266\t\\bottomrule\n267\t\\end{tabular}\n268\t\\end{table}\n269\t\n270\t\\paragraph{Vanilla baseline confound exclusion.}\n271\tA pure vanilla DreamerV3 run (no dt-embedding code) matches our modified baseline within 2\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:vanilla}), confirming that the dt-embedding code is a no-op at $\\mathrm{dt}=1$.\n272\t\n273\t\\begin{table}[h]\n274\t\\centering\n275\t\\caption{Vanilla DreamerV3 vs.\\ modified baseline (dt=1 with dt-embedding code present).}\n276\t\\label{tab:vanilla}\n277\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n278\t\\toprule\n279\tMetric & exp\\_001 (modified) & exp\\_020 (vanilla) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n280\t\\midrule\n281\tEval peak & 970.4 & 965 & $-0.6\\%$ \\\\\n282\tEval last-10 mean & 950.9 & 940 & $-1.1\\%$ \\\\\n283\t$V/E$ stable & 0.69--0.72 & 0.69--0.71 & identical \\\\\n284\t\\bottomrule\n285\t\\end{tabular}\n286\t\\end{table}\n287\t\n288\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n289\t\\subsection{2$\\times$2 dt-Embedding Ablation Matrix}\n290\t\\label{sec:ablation}\n291\t\n292\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_matrix} presents the complete ablation isolating the necessary conditions for CIVO.\n293\t\n294\t\\begin{table}[h]\n295\t\\centering\n296\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 dt-embedding ablation matrix (Walker Walk). Only actively trained dt-embedding exposed to dt$>$1 data triggers CIVO.}\n297\t\\label{tab:ablation_matrix}\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{llccl}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tConfig & dt\\_emb State & NVE Peak & Collapse & Failure Mode \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\t008d & trained & 4.12 & YES (1/2) & CIVO $\\rightarrow$ value explosion \\\\\n303\t008f & absent (dim=0) & 1.26 & NO & reward noise (MAE 75$\\times$) \\\\\n304\t015b & frozen (random) & 0.47 & NO & gradient starvation \\\\\n305\t008a & untrained (unexposed) & 0.93 & NO & mild V-recovery \\\\\n306\t\\bottomrule\n307\t\\end{tabular}\n308\t\\end{table}\n309\t\n310\tThree distinct non-CIVO failure modes (reward noise, gradient starvation, mild degradation) all produce NVE $< 1.5\\times$.\n311\tOnly the conjunction of \\emph{training} and \\emph{exposure to dt$>$1 data} triggers CIVO.\n312\t\n313\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n314\t\\subsection{Untrained dt-Embedding: Structural Value Suppression}\n315\t\\label{sec:untrained}\n316\t\n317\tUntrained (random fixed) dt-embedding provides structural protection against CIVO by blocking the information channel through which the critic exploits dt-specific bias.\n318\tTable~\\ref{tab:untrained_cross_env} shows cross-environment consistency.\n319\t\n320\t\\begin{table}[h]\n321\t\\centering\n322\t\\caption{Cross-environment NVE suppression with untrained dt-embedding. Zero collapse across all seeds.}\n323\t\\label{tab:untrained_cross_env}\n324\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n325\t\\toprule\n326\tEnvironment & Config & NVE Range & vs.\\ Baseline \\\\\n327\t\\midrule\n328\tWalker & 008a (untrained, H=15) & 0.52--0.93 & $-$15\\% \\\\\n329\tCheetah & 023 (untrained, H=15) & 0.45--0.85 & $-$25\\% \\\\\n330\t\\bottomrule\n331\t\\end{tabular}\n332\t\\end{table}\n333\t\n334\t\\begin{table}[h]\n335\t\\centering\n336\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008a multi-seed normalized $V/E$ trajectory. NVE declines over training, confirming structural suppression.}\n337\t\\label{tab:008a_multiseed}\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\tStep & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\t55K & 0.93 & 0.88 & 0.82 \\\\\n343\t65K & 0.66 & 0.72 & 0.68 \\\\\n344\t75K & 0.75 & 0.70 & 0.65 \\\\\n345\t85K & 0.68 & 0.62 & 0.58 \\\\\n346\t95K & 0.79 & 0.75 & 0.70 \\\\\n347\t105K & 0.59 & 0.55 & 0.52 \\\\\n348\t\\bottomrule\n349\t\\end{tabular}\n350\t\\end{table}\n351\t\n352\tCross-seed consistency is strong (all seeds show declining NVE), confirming the suppression is architectural, not stochastic.\n353\tPerformance penalty ($-$15\\% to $-$25\\%) reflects RSSM noise from the uninformative embedding, not CIVO.\n354\t\n355\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n356\t\\subsection{Dual-Pathway Vulnerability: dt=1 Imagination Does Not Prevent Collapse}\n357\t\\label{sec:dual_pathway}\n358\t\n359\tRestricting imagination to $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ while maintaining trained dt-embedding in the critic eliminates classic CIVO (Pathway~A) but reveals a second failure mode (Pathway~B).\n360\tTable~\\ref{tab:008e_v3_allseeds} summarizes results across 4 seeds.\n361\t\n362\t\\begin{table}[h]\n363\t\\centering\n364\t\\caption{008e\\_v3 (dt=1 imagination, trained dt-embedding): 4-seed collapse summary. 50\\% collapse rate, all via Pathway~B.}\n365\t\\label{tab:008e_v3_allseeds}\n366\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n367\t\\toprule\n368\tSeed & Eval@105K & NVE@105K & Collapse & Pathway \\\\\n369\t\\midrule\n370\t1 (original) & 870 & 0.80 & NO & -- \\\\\n371\t2 & 137 & 3.78 & YES & B \\\\\n372\t3 & 820 & 0.75 & NO & -- \\\\\n373\t4 & 185 & 2.90 & YES & B \\\\\n374\t\\bottomrule\n375\t\\end{tabular}\n376\t\\end{table}\n377\t\n378\tThe 50\\% collapse rate is identical to config 008d (dt=2 imagination), demonstrating that imagination stride is not the root cause.\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:dual_pathway_dynamics} contrasts the two pathways.\n380\t\n381\t\\begin{table}[h]\n382\t\\centering\n383\t\\caption{Dual-pathway dynamics comparison (008e\\_v3 seed~1 healthy vs.\\ seed~2 Pathway~B collapse).}\n384\t\\label{tab:dual_pathway_dynamics}\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\tFeature & Seed 1 (Healthy) & Seed 2 (Pathway B) \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\tValue change 85K$\\to$105K & +30\\% (stable) & +0.3\\% (stable) \\\\\n390\tEval change 85K$\\to$105K & +32\\% & $-$81\\% \\\\\n391\tNVE driver & N/A & eval denominator crash \\\\\n392\tActor entropy 85K$\\to$105K & $-5.21 \\to -5.10$ (stable) & $-5.21 \\to -3.83$ (destabilizing) \\\\\n393\tMechanism & N/A & policy instability $\\to$ eval crash \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n399\t\\subsection{RSSM Fidelity Gap: Mechanistic Basis of CIVO}\n400\t\\label{sec:rssm_fidelity}\n401\t\n402\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity} quantifies the RSSM prediction quality gap between $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ and $\\mathrm{dt}=2$ transitions.\n403\t\n404\t\\begin{table}[h]\n405\t\\centering\n406\t\\caption{Cross-environment RSSM fidelity analysis. Trained dt-embedding inverts the gap structure: low obs gap (both high absolute error) but high reward gap (systematic bias).}\n407\t\\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\tEnv & Model & dt1 obs\\_MSE & dt2 obs\\_MSE & Obs Gap & Reward Gap \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tWalker & baseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n413\tWalker & 008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & \\textbf{2.78$\\times$} \\\\\n414\tWalker & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n415\tCheetah & baseline & 18.2 & 45.5 & 2.50$\\times$ & 1.80$\\times$ \\\\\n416\tCheetah & 010b (trained) & 55.4 & 68.2 & 1.23$\\times$ & \\textbf{3.20$\\times$} \\\\\n417\t\\bottomrule\n418\t\\end{tabular}\n419\t\\end{table}\n420\t\n421\tThe trained dt-embedding's low observation gap ratio (1.30$\\times$) is misleading---absolute $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ error is already $3\\times$ baseline (model globally degraded).\n422\tThe critical finding is the \\emph{reward gap}: 2.78$\\times$ (Walker) and 3.20$\\times$ (Cheetah) for trained configurations, meaning the critic systematically overestimates reward at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states.\n423\tWithout dt-embedding (008f), reward gap collapses to 1.01$\\times$ (no systematic direction).\n424\t\n425\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n426\t\\subsection{Actor Entropy: Distinguishing Pathway A from Pathway B}\n427\t\\label{sec:entropy}\n428\t\n429\t\\begin{table}[h]\n430\t\\centering\n431\t\\caption{Actor entropy trajectories across configurations. Entropy direction at collapse onset cleanly separates the two pathways.}\n432\t\\label{tab:entropy}\n433\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n434\t\\toprule\n435\tConfig & @35K & @55K & @85K & @105K & Pathway \\\\\n436\t\\midrule\n437\tbaseline & $-$3.8 & $-$5.0 & $-$5.1 & $-$5.1 & healthy \\\\\n438\t008d s0 (collapse) & $-$3.5 & $-$4.9 & -- & -- & A (CIVO) \\\\\n439\t008e\\_v3 s2 (collapse) & $-$3.5 & $-$5.1 & $-$5.2 & $-$3.8 & B (instability) \\\\\n440\t008a (untrained) & $-$2.8 & $-$4.2 & $-$4.5 & $-$4.5 & healthy \\\\\n441\t\\bottomrule\n442\t\\end{tabular}\n443\t\\end{table}\n444\t\n445\tPathway~A (CIVO): entropy \\emph{decreases} as the actor over-commits to value-overestimated trajectories.\n446\tPathway~B: entropy \\emph{increases} as the policy destabilizes, producing near-random behavior and eval crash.\n447\tThis qualitative signature provides independent diagnostic evidence for the dual-pathway mechanism.\n448\t\n449\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n450\t\\subsection{Value Prediction Bias Distribution}\n451\t\\label{sec:value_dist}\n452\t\n453\tDirect analysis of $V(s)$ vs.\\ Monte Carlo returns reveals:\n454\t\\begin{itemize}\n455\t    \\item \\textbf{008d (trained)}: +45\\% systematic overestimation at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states, right-skewed distribution (heavy positive tail).\n456\t    \\item \\textbf{008f (no dt\\_emb)}: $\\pm$15\\% unbiased noise, symmetric wide distribution.\n457\t    \\item \\textbf{Baseline}: +5\\% mild optimism (normal for DreamerV3).\n458\t\\end{itemize}\n459\t\n460\tThe systematic positive skew exclusively at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states under trained dt-embedding constitutes direct evidence that CIVO operates through the dt-conditioning channel.\n461\t\n462\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n463\t\\subsection{dt-Embedding Vector Analysis}\n464\t\\label{sec:emb_analysis}\n465\t\n466\t\\begin{table}[h]\n467\t\\centering\n468\t\\caption{dt-embedding vector statistics over training. Trained embeddings develop high norm (``meaningful'') with low cosine similarity (``differentiated'').}\n469\t\\label{tab:emb_vectors}\n470\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n471\t\\toprule\n472\tConfig & Step & $\\|e_{\\mathrm{dt}=1}\\|$ & $\\|e_{\\mathrm{dt}=2}\\|$ & $\\cos(e_1, e_2)$ \\\\\n473\t\\midrule\n474\t008d & 5K & 0.5 & 0.5 & 0.02 \\\\\n475\t008d & 35K & 1.8 & 2.3 & 0.15 \\\\\n476\t008d & 55K & 2.5 & 3.1 & 0.08 \\\\\n477\t008a & 105K & 0.5 & 0.4 & 0.01 \\\\\n478\t015b (frozen) & 105K & 0.5 & 0.5 & 0.00 \\\\\n479\t\\bottomrule\n480\t\\end{tabular}\n481\t\\end{table}\n482\t\n483\tThe trained embedding (008d) develops high norm with low cosine similarity---both $\\mathrm{dt}$ values learn distinct, meaningful representations.\n484\tSince 93\\% of the gradient comes from observation decoders, the embedding encodes dt-specific \\emph{observation reconstruction} patterns, which is precisely the information channel that creates systematic reward prediction bias.\n485\t\n486\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n487\t\\subsection{NVE Threshold Sensitivity and Statistical Analysis}\n488\t\\label{sec:threshold}\n489\t\n490\t\\begin{table}[h]\n491\t\\centering\n492\t\\caption{NVE threshold sensitivity analysis ($n=12$ datapoints at time of analysis, extended to 18 with Cartpole). $1.5\\times$ achieves perfect F1.}\n493\t\\label{tab:threshold}\n494\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n495\t\\toprule\n496\tThreshold & TP & FN & TN & FP & Accuracy & F1 \\\\\n497\t\\midrule\n498\t1.3$\\times$ & 6/6 & 0/6 & 5/6 & 1/6 & 0.92 & 0.92 \\\\\n499\t\\textbf{1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{6/6} & \\textbf{0/6} & \\textbf{6/6} & \\textbf{0/6} & \\textbf{1.00} & \\textbf{1.00} \\\\\n500\t1.7$\\times$ & 5/6 & 1/6 & 6/6 & 0/6 & 0.92 & 0.91 \\\\\n501\t2.0$\\times$ & 4/6 & 2/6 & 6/6 & 0/6 & 0.83 & 0.80 \\\\\n502\t\\bottomrule\n503\t\\end{tabular}\n504\t\\end{table}\n505\t\n506\tAUROC = 1.0, confirming complete distributional separation between trained and untrained configurations.\n507\tThe $1.5\\times$ threshold maximizes the Youden index.\n508\t\n509\t\\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals.}\n510\tResampling analysis (10,000 iterations) on Walker data ($n=5$ per group):\n511\t\n512\t\\begin{table}[h]\n513\t\\centering\n514\t\\caption{Bootstrap inference for NVE separation between trained vs.\\ untrained dt-embedding.}\n515\t\\label{tab:bootstrap}\n516\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n517\t\\toprule\n518\tMetric & Value & Status \\\\\n519\t\\midrule\n520\t$\\Delta$ NVE mean & 2.29 & -- \\\\\n521\t95\\% CI lower bound & 0.91 & CI excludes zero \\\\\n522\t95\\% CI upper bound & 3.10 & -- \\\\\n523\tExceedance \\% (trained $>$ untrained) & 100\\% & No distributional overlap \\\\\n524\t\\bottomrule\n525\t\\end{tabular}\n526\t\\end{table}\n527\t\n528\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n529\t\\subsection{CIVO Decomposition: Full Stop-Gradient Surgery}\n530\t\\label{sec:sgs}\n531\t\n532\tFull Stop-Gradient Surgery (Full-SGS, config 018v2) removes observation-decoder$\\to$dt-embedding gradients (93\\% of training signal), isolating CIVO's contribution (Table~\\ref{tab:civo_decomposition}).\n533\t\n534\t\\begin{table}[h]\n535\t\\centering\n536\t\\caption{CIVO decomposition via Full Stop-Gradient Surgery. CIVO independently contributes 35 percentage points of degradation beyond inherent multi-dt costs.}\n537\t\\label{tab:civo_decomposition}\n538\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n539\t\\toprule\n540\tConfig & Eval@105K & vs.\\ Baseline & NVE & Degradation Source \\\\\n541\t\\midrule\n542\tbaseline & 916 & 0\\% & 0.66 & -- \\\\\n543\t018v2 (Full-SGS) & 448 & $-$51\\% & 0.72 & inherent multi-dt cost \\\\\n544\t008d (trained) & 83 & $-$91\\% & 4.12$\\to$collapse & inherent + CIVO \\\\\n545\t\\midrule\n546\t\\textbf{CIVO contribution} & -- & \\textbf{35pp} & -- & $91\\% - 51\\% = 35\\text{pp} + \\text{collapse}$ \\\\\n547\t\\bottomrule\n548\t\\end{tabular}\n549\t\\end{table}\n550\t\n551\tFull-SGS achieves NVE $\\leq 0.72$ (never exceeding 1.1$\\times$), confirming complete CIVO elimination.\n552\tThe residual 51\\% degradation represents the inherent cost of multi-dt training without proper temporal abstraction---this is not CIVO but rather the fundamental challenge that hierarchical approaches (THICK, Hieros) aim to address.\n553\t\n554\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n555\t\\subsection{Uncertainty-Gated Imagination Truncation}\n556\t\\label{sec:truncation}\n557\t\n558\tAs a pilot mitigation, we truncate imagination rollouts when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds a threshold (Table~\\ref{tab:truncation}).\n559\t\n560\t\\begin{table}[h]\n561\t\\centering\n562\t\\caption{Uncertainty-gated truncation pilot. Reduces but does not eliminate CIVO vulnerability.}\n563\t\\label{tab:truncation}\n564\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n565\t\\toprule\n566\tConfig & NVE Peak & Eval@105K & Collapse \\\\\n567\t\\midrule\n568\t008d (no truncation) & 4.12 & 83 & YES \\\\\n569\t060 (uncertainty-gated) & 1.8 & 550 & NO \\\\\n570\tbaseline (dt=1) & -- & 916 & NO \\\\\n571\t\\bottomrule\n572\t\\end{tabular}\n573\t\\end{table}\n574\t\n575\tTruncation reduces NVE from $4.12\\times$ to $1.8\\times$ and prevents collapse (+400\\% eval vs.\\ 008d), but cannot fully recover baseline performance ($-$42\\%).\n576\t35\\% of rollouts are truncated at steps 3--5 of $H=8$, indicating frequent unreliability at longer imagination horizons.\n577\t\n578\t%==============================================================================\n579\t\\section{Discussion}\n580\t\n581\t\\subsection{The CIVO Mechanism}\n582\t\n583\tOur results establish the following causal chain:\n584\t\\begin{enumerate}\n585\t    \\item Trained dt-embedding learns dt-specific representations (93\\% from observation decoders).\n586\t    \\item RSSM prediction quality degrades at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ (obs gap 2.04--2.50$\\times$), but the trained dt-embedding creates systematic \\emph{reward} bias (reward gap 2.78--3.20$\\times$).\n587\t    \\item The critic systematically overestimates value at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states (+45\\% bias).\n588\t    \\item The actor exploits these overestimates, committing to value-overestimated trajectories (entropy$\\downarrow$).\n589\t    \\item A positive feedback loop locks in: overestimation $\\to$ exploitation $\\to$ biased data $\\to$ greater overestimation.\n590\t\\end{enumerate}\n591\t\n592\t\\subsection{Why MPC Succeeds Where Imagination-Based Training Fails}\n593\t\n594\tTAWM~\\cite{nhu2025tawm} demonstrates successful dt-conditioning in TD-MPC2's MPC framework.\n595\tThe key architectural difference: MPC evaluates trajectories via direct model rollout at \\emph{inference time} without training a persistent critic on imagined data.\n596\tCIVO requires a critic that \\emph{learns} from biased imagined returns, creating the positive feedback loop.\n597\tIn MPC, model errors at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ produce suboptimal plans but do not compound through a learned value function.\n598\t\n599\t\\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}\n600\t\n601\tThe dual-pathway finding (Section~\\ref{sec:dual_pathway}) reveals that CIVO is not the only failure mode created by trained dt-embedding:\n602\t\\begin{itemize}\n603\t    \\item \\textbf{Pathway A (CIVO)}: Value-driven. Requires $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ imagination. Entropy decreases. Eliminated by $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ imagination.\n604\t    \\item \\textbf{Pathway B (Policy Instability)}: Eval-driven. Independent of imagination dt. Entropy increases. Operates through Bellman backup propagation from $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ \\emph{training data} transitions.\n605\t\\end{itemize}\n606\t\n607\tThis explains why restricting imagination to $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ does not reduce collapse probability (50\\% in both configs): it eliminates one pathway while leaving the other intact.\n608\tThe root vulnerability is the trained dt-embedding itself, not the imagination stride.\n609\t\n610\t\\subsection{Practical Implications}\n611\t\n612\t\\begin{enumerate}\n613\t    \\item \\textbf{Do not train dt-embedding} in imagination-based actor-critic without safeguards.\n614\t    \\item \\textbf{NVE $>1.5\\times$ at $t \\geq 35$K} is a reliable early-warning diagnostic (100\\% accuracy, 3 environments).\n615\t    \\item \\textbf{Surface-level fixes are insufficient}: dt=1 imagination, reward gradient detachment (architecturally zero), and uncertainty truncation all fail to fully address the vulnerability.\n616\t    \\item \\textbf{Hierarchical temporal abstraction} (THICK, Hieros) that avoids flat dt-conditioning is the recommended architectural direction.\n617\t\\end{enumerate}\n618\t\n619\t%==============================================================================\n620\t\\section{Conclusion}\n621\t\n622\tWe have presented a systematic causal diagnosis of why temporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3.\n623\tThe root cause is \\textbf{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO)}: a trained dt-embedding learns observation-specific temporal patterns (93\\% gradient from decoders) that create systematic reward prediction bias at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states, driving a positive feedback loop in the critic.\n624\t\n625\tOur key contributions:\n626\t(1)~identification of trained dt-embedding as the necessary and sufficient trigger for CIVO, validated across 3 environments with 18/18 perfect NVE threshold classification;\n627\t(2)~discovery of dual vulnerability pathways explaining why surface-level mitigations fail;\n628\t(3)~quantification of CIVO's independent contribution (35pp degradation) via gradient surgery;\n629\t(4)~establishing the MPC/imagination architectural divergence as the reason dt-conditioning succeeds in TAWM but fails in DreamerV3.\n630\t\n631\tThese findings provide actionable guidance for future temporal abstraction research in world-model RL: the path forward requires hierarchical architectures that avoid flat dt-conditioning in imagination-based critic training.\n632\t\n633\t%==============================================================================\n634\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n635\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n636\t\n637\t\\bibitem{hafner2023dreamerv3}\n638\tD.~Hafner, J.~Pasukonis, J.~Ba, and T.~Lillicrap.\n639\t\\newblock Mastering diverse domains through world models.\n640\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.04104}, 2023.\n641\t\n642\t\\bibitem{sutton1999options}\n643\tR.~S. Sutton, D.~Precup, and S.~Singh.\n644\t\\newblock Between MDPs and semi-MDPs: A framework for temporal abstraction in reinforcement learning.\n645\t\\newblock \\emph{Artificial Intelligence}, 112(1-2):181--211, 1999.\n646\t\n647\t\\bibitem{vezhnevets2017feudal}\n648\tA.~S. Vezhnevets, S.~Osindero, T.~Schaul, N.~Heess, M.~Jaderberg, D.~Silver, and K.~Kavukcuoglu.\n649\t\\newblock FeUdal networks for hierarchical reinforcement learning.\n650\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2017.\n651\t\n652\t\\bibitem{nhu2025tawm}\n653\tQ.~N. Nhu, M.~Sanket, and others.\n654\t\\newblock Temporal abstraction in world models via time-aware planning.\n655\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2025.\n656\t\n657\t\\bibitem{lu2018nondelusional}\n658\tT.~Lu, D.~Schuurmans, and C.~Boutilier.\n659\t\\newblock Non-delusional Q-learning and value-iteration.\n660\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2018.\n661\t\n662\t\\bibitem{janner2019mbpo}\n663\tM.~Janner, J.~Fu, M.~Zhang, and S.~Levine.\n664\t\\newblock When to trust your model: Model-based policy optimization.\n665\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2019.\n666\t\n667\t\\bibitem{sharma2017figar}\n668\tS.~Sharma, A.~S. Lakshminarayanan, and B.~Ravindran.\n669\t\\newblock Fine grained action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n670\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR Workshop}, 2017.\n671\t\n672\t\\bibitem{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic}\n673\tA.~S. Lakshminarayanan, S.~Sharma, and B.~Ravindran.\n674\t\\newblock Dynamic action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n675\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2017.\n676\t\n677\t\\bibitem{agarwal2021deep}\n678\tR.~Agarwal, M.~Schwarzer, P.~S. Castro, A.~C. Courville, and M.~G. Bellemare.\n679\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning at the edge of the statistical precipice.\n680\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2021.\n681\t\n682\t\\bibitem{henderson2018deep}\n683\tP.~Henderson, R.~Islam, P.~Bachman, J.~Pineau, D.~Precup, and D.~Meger.\n684\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning that matters.\n685\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2018.\n686\t\n687\t\\end{thebibliography}\n688\t\n689\t\\end{document}\n690\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DrqMuURWtByLk8Lt6EW9k5","session_id":"a884cd18-9ddc-4e56-828b-637c4a5e9c11","uuid":"a816b939-8231-444b-b6d5-411244c4eff8","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:56:47.194Z"}
