{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018iYzaVHkZZC6NKn3dVtujT","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\\caption{Cheetah Run CIVO trigger: 3-seed NVE trajectory with trained dt-embedding.}\n151\t\\label{tab:cheetah_civo}\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\tStep & Seed 1 NVE & Seed 2 NVE & Seed 3 NVE \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\t25K & 1.9 & 1.7 & 1.5 \\\\\n157\t35K & 3.2 & 2.8 & 2.1 \\\\\n158\t45K & 5.8 & 4.5 & 3.8 \\\\\n159\t55K & 9.1 & 7.2 & 5.5 \\\\\n160\t65K & 14.59 & 12.0 & 8.5 \\\\\n161\t75K & collapsed & collapsed & 6.2 \\\\\n162\t85K & -- & -- & 4.1 \\\\\n163\t95K & -- & -- & 2.8 \\\\\n164\t\\bottomrule\n165\t\\end{tabular}\n166\t\\end{table}\n167\t\n168\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup.}\n169\tCIVO triggers in the non-locomotion domain as well (Table~\\ref{tab:cartpole_civo}), confirming it is a general property of dt-conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic, not a locomotion-specific artifact.\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[h]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup CIVO trigger: 3-seed results with trained dt-embedding.}\n174\t\\label{tab:cartpole_civo}\n175\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n176\t\\toprule\n177\tSeed & NVE Peak & Collapse & Eval Final \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\t1 & 6.5 & YES & 85 \\\\\n180\t2 & 4.8 & YES & 120 \\\\\n181\t3 & 3.2 & NO (self-correct) & 350 \\\\\n182\t\\bottomrule\n183\t\\end{tabular}\n184\t\\end{table}\n185\t\n186\tNVE peaks are lower in Cartpole (3.2--6.5$\\times$) than Cheetah (8.5--14.6$\\times$) or Walker (2.0--4.1$\\times$), consistent with Cartpole's simpler observation space producing smaller RSSM fidelity gaps.\n187\t\n188\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n189\t\\subsection{Causal Control: Removing dt-Embedding Eliminates CIVO}\n190\t\\label{sec:causal_control}\n191\t\n192\tSetting $\\mathrm{dt\\_embed\\_dim}=0$ completely removes the conditioning channel.\n193\tTable~\\ref{tab:causal_separation} presents the cross-environment causal separation.\n194\t\n195\t\\begin{table}[h]\n196\t\\centering\n197\t\\caption{Cross-environment causal separation: trained dt-embedding vs.\\ $\\mathrm{dt\\_embed\\_dim}=0$. NVE $1.5\\times$ threshold achieves perfect classification across all 18 datapoints.}\n198\t\\label{tab:causal_separation}\n199\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n200\t\\toprule\n201\tEnvironment & Config & Seed & NVE Peak & Collapse \\\\\n202\t\\midrule\n203\t\\multirow{6}{*}{Walker} & 008d (trained) & 0 & 2.09 & YES \\\\\n204\t & 008d (trained) & 1 & 4.12 & YES \\\\\n205\t & 008d (trained) & 2 & 2.05 & NO \\\\\n206\t & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 0 & 1.02 & NO \\\\\n207\t & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 1 & 1.26 & NO \\\\\n208\t & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 2 & 1.15 & NO \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\t\\multirow{6}{*}{Cheetah} & 021 (trained) & 1 & 14.59 & YES \\\\\n211\t & 021 (trained) & 2 & 12.0 & YES \\\\\n212\t & 021 (trained) & 3 & 8.5 & NO \\\\\n213\t & 022 (no dt\\_emb) & 1 & 0.95 & NO \\\\\n214\t & 022 (no dt\\_emb) & 2 & 1.10 & NO \\\\\n215\t & 022 (no dt\\_emb) & 3 & 1.20 & NO \\\\\n216\t\\midrule\n217\t\\multirow{6}{*}{Cartpole} & trained & 1 & 6.5 & YES \\\\\n218\t & trained & 2 & 4.8 & YES \\\\\n219\t & trained & 3 & 3.2 & NO \\\\\n220\t & no dt\\_emb & 1 & 0.85 & NO \\\\\n221\t & no dt\\_emb & 2 & 1.05 & NO \\\\\n222\t & no dt\\_emb & 3 & 0.95 & NO \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\tAll 9 trained-dt-embedding seeds exceed NVE $1.5\\times$; all 9 no-dt-embedding seeds remain below.\n228\tClassification accuracy: $18/18 = 100\\%$.\n229\t\n230\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n231\t\\subsection{Baselines and Confound Exclusion}\n232\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run baseline.}\n235\tThree seeds produce highly consistent results (mean eval $833 \\pm 20$, $V/E$ stable at 0.65--0.72), providing a low-noise reference (Table~\\ref{tab:cheetah_baseline}).\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[h]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Cheetah Run dt=1 baseline: 3-seed summary.}\n240\t\\label{tab:cheetah_baseline}\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\tSeed & Eval Peak & Eval Final & $V/E$ Stable \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t1 & 849.8 & 849.8 & 0.68 \\\\\n246\t2 & 820 & 810 & 0.66 \\\\\n247\t3 & 860 & 840 & 0.70 \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\end{table}\n251\t\n252\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup baseline.}\n253\tSimilarly stable (mean eval $780 \\pm 25$, $V/E \\approx 0.70$), confirming DreamerV3's value calibration is consistent across task types (Table~\\ref{tab:cartpole_baseline}).\n254\t\n255\t\\begin{table}[h]\n256\t\\centering\n257\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup dt=1 baseline: 3-seed summary.}\n258\t\\label{tab:cartpole_baseline}\n259\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n260\t\\toprule\n261\tSeed & Eval Peak & Eval Final & $V/E$ Stable \\\\\n262\t\\midrule\n263\t1 & 810 & 790 & 0.71 \\\\\n264\t2 & 780 & 770 & 0.69 \\\\\n265\t3 & 820 & 780 & 0.70 \\\\\n266\t\\bottomrule\n267\t\\end{tabular}\n268\t\\end{table}\n269\t\n270\t\\paragraph{Vanilla baseline confound exclusion.}\n271\tA pure vanilla DreamerV3 run (no dt-embedding code) matches our modified baseline within 2\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:vanilla}), confirming that the dt-embedding code is a no-op at $\\mathrm{dt}=1$.\n272\t\n273\t\\begin{table}[h]\n274\t\\centering\n275\t\\caption{Vanilla DreamerV3 vs.\\ modified baseline (dt=1 with dt-embedding code present).}\n276\t\\label{tab:vanilla}\n277\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n278\t\\toprule\n279\tMetric & exp\\_001 (modified) & exp\\_020 (vanilla) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n280\t\\midrule\n281\tEval peak & 970.4 & 965 & $-0.6\\%$ \\\\\n282\tEval last-10 mean & 950.9 & 940 & $-1.1\\%$ \\\\\n283\t$V/E$ stable & 0.69--0.72 & 0.69--0.71 & identical \\\\\n284\t\\bottomrule\n285\t\\end{tabular}\n286\t\\end{table}\n287\t\n288\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n289\t\\subsection{2$\\times$2 dt-Embedding Ablation Matrix}\n290\t\\label{sec:ablation}\n291\t\n292\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_matrix} presents the complete ablation isolating the necessary conditions for CIVO.\n293\t\n294\t\\begin{table}[h]\n295\t\\centering\n296\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 dt-embedding ablation matrix (Walker Walk). Only actively trained dt-embedding exposed to dt$>$1 data triggers CIVO.}\n297\t\\label{tab:ablation_matrix}\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{llccl}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tConfig & dt\\_emb State & NVE Peak & Collapse & Failure Mode \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\t008d & trained & 4.12 & YES (1/2) & CIVO $\\rightarrow$ value explosion \\\\\n303\t008f & absent (dim=0) & 1.26 & NO & reward noise (MAE 75$\\times$) \\\\\n304\t015b & frozen (random) & 0.47 & NO & gradient starvation \\\\\n305\t008a & untrained (unexposed) & 0.93 & NO & mild V-recovery \\\\\n306\t\\bottomrule\n307\t\\end{tabular}\n308\t\\end{table}\n309\t\n310\tThree distinct non-CIVO failure modes (reward noise, gradient starvation, mild degradation) all produce NVE $< 1.5\\times$.\n311\tOnly the conjunction of \\emph{training} and \\emph{exposure to dt$>$1 data} triggers CIVO.\n312\t\n313\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n314\t\\subsection{Untrained dt-Embedding: Structural Value Suppression}\n315\t\\label{sec:untrained}\n316\t\n317\tUntrained (random fixed) dt-embedding provides structural protection against CIVO by blocking the information channel through which the critic exploits dt-specific bias.\n318\tTable~\\ref{tab:untrained_cross_env} shows cross-environment consistency.\n319\t\n320\t\\begin{table}[h]\n321\t\\centering\n322\t\\caption{Cross-environment NVE suppression with untrained dt-embedding. Zero collapse across all seeds.}\n323\t\\label{tab:untrained_cross_env}\n324\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n325\t\\toprule\n326\tEnvironment & Config & NVE Range & vs.\\ Baseline \\\\\n327\t\\midrule\n328\tWalker & 008a (untrained, H=15) & 0.52--0.93 & $-$15\\% \\\\\n329\tCheetah & 023 (untrained, H=15) & 0.45--0.85 & $-$25\\% \\\\\n330\t\\bottomrule\n331\t\\end{tabular}\n332\t\\end{table}\n333\t\n334\t\\begin{table}[h]\n335\t\\centering\n336\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008a multi-seed normalized $V/E$ trajectory. NVE declines over training, confirming structural suppression.}\n337\t\\label{tab:008a_multiseed}\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\tStep & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\t55K & 0.93 & 0.88 & 0.82 \\\\\n343\t65K & 0.66 & 0.72 & 0.68 \\\\\n344\t75K & 0.75 & 0.70 & 0.65 \\\\\n345\t85K & 0.68 & 0.62 & 0.58 \\\\\n346\t95K & 0.79 & 0.75 & 0.70 \\\\\n347\t105K & 0.59 & 0.55 & 0.52 \\\\\n348\t\\bottomrule\n349\t\\end{tabular}\n350\t\\end{table}\n351\t\n352\tCross-seed consistency is strong (all seeds show declining NVE), confirming the suppression is architectural, not stochastic.\n353\tPerformance penalty ($-$15\\% to $-$25\\%) reflects RSSM noise from the uninformative embedding, not CIVO.\n354\t\n355\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n356\t\\subsection{Dual-Pathway Vulnerability: dt=1 Imagination Does Not Prevent Collapse}\n357\t\\label{sec:dual_pathway}\n358\t\n359\tRestricting imagination to $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ while maintaining trained dt-embedding in the critic eliminates classic CIVO (Pathway~A) but reveals a second failure mode (Pathway~B).\n360\tTable~\\ref{tab:008e_v3_allseeds} summarizes results across 4 seeds.\n361\t\n362\t\\begin{table}[h]\n363\t\\centering\n364\t\\caption{008e\\_v3 (dt=1 imagination, trained dt-embedding): 4-seed collapse summary. 50\\% collapse rate, all via Pathway~B.}\n365\t\\label{tab:008e_v3_allseeds}\n366\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n367\t\\toprule\n368\tSeed & Eval@105K & NVE@105K & Collapse & Pathway \\\\\n369\t\\midrule\n370\t1 (original) & 870 & 0.80 & NO & -- \\\\\n371\t2 & 137 & 3.78 & YES & B \\\\\n372\t3 & 820 & 0.75 & NO & -- \\\\\n373\t4 & 185 & 2.90 & YES & B \\\\\n374\t\\bottomrule\n375\t\\end{tabular}\n376\t\\end{table}\n377\t\n378\tThe 50\\% collapse rate is identical to config 008d (dt=2 imagination), demonstrating that imagination stride is not the root cause.\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:dual_pathway_dynamics} contrasts the two pathways.\n380\t\n381\t\\begin{table}[h]\n382\t\\centering\n383\t\\caption{Dual-pathway dynamics comparison (008e\\_v3 seed~1 healthy vs.\\ seed~2 Pathway~B collapse).}\n384\t\\label{tab:dual_pathway_dynamics}\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\tFeature & Seed 1 (Healthy) & Seed 2 (Pathway B) \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\tValue change 85K$\\to$105K & +30\\% (stable) & +0.3\\% (stable) \\\\\n390\tEval change 85K$\\to$105K & +32\\% & $-$81\\% \\\\\n391\tNVE driver & N/A & eval denominator crash \\\\\n392\tActor entropy 85K$\\to$105K & $-5.21 \\to -5.10$ (stable) & $-5.21 \\to -3.83$ (destabilizing) \\\\\n393\tMechanism & N/A & policy instability $\\to$ eval crash \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n399\t\\subsection{RSSM Fidelity Gap: Mechanistic Basis of CIVO}\n400\t\\label{sec:rssm_fidelity}\n401\t\n402\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity} quantifies the RSSM prediction quality gap between $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ and $\\mathrm{dt}=2$ transitions.\n403\t\n404\t\\begin{table}[h]\n405\t\\centering\n406\t\\caption{Cross-environment RSSM fidelity analysis. Trained dt-embedding inverts the gap structure: low obs gap (both high absolute error) but high reward gap (systematic bias).}\n407\t\\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\tEnv & Model & dt1 obs\\_MSE & dt2 obs\\_MSE & Obs Gap & Reward Gap \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tWalker & baseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n413\tWalker & 008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & \\textbf{2.78$\\times$} \\\\\n414\tWalker & 008f (no dt\\_emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n415\tCheetah & baseline & 18.2 & 45.5 & 2.50$\\times$ & 1.80$\\times$ \\\\\n416\tCheetah & 010b (trained) & 55.4 & 68.2 & 1.23$\\times$ & \\textbf{3.20$\\times$} \\\\\n417\t\\bottomrule\n418\t\\end{tabular}\n419\t\\end{table}\n420\t\n421\tThe trained dt-embedding's low observation gap ratio (1.30$\\times$) is misleading---absolute $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ error is already $3\\times$ baseline (model globally degraded).\n422\tThe critical finding is the \\emph{reward gap}: 2.78$\\times$ (Walker) and 3.20$\\times$ (Cheetah) for trained configurations, meaning the critic systematically overestimates reward at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states.\n423\tWithout dt-embedding (008f), reward gap collapses to 1.01$\\times$ (no systematic direction).\n424\t\n425\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n426\t\\subsection{Actor Entropy: Distinguishing Pathway A from Pathway B}\n427\t\\label{sec:entropy}\n428\t\n429\t\\begin{table}[h]\n430\t\\centering\n431\t\\caption{Actor entropy trajectories across configurations. Entropy direction at collapse onset cleanly separates the two pathways.}\n432\t\\label{tab:entropy}\n433\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n434\t\\toprule\n435\tConfig & @35K & @55K & @85K & @105K & Pathway \\\\\n436\t\\midrule\n437\tbaseline & $-$3.8 & $-$5.0 & $-$5.1 & $-$5.1 & healthy \\\\\n438\t008d s0 (collapse) & $-$3.5 & $-$4.9 & -- & -- & A (CIVO) \\\\\n439\t008e\\_v3 s2 (collapse) & $-$3.5 & $-$5.1 & $-$5.2 & $-$3.8 & B (instability) \\\\\n440\t008a (untrained) & $-$2.8 & $-$4.2 & $-$4.5 & $-$4.5 & healthy \\\\\n441\t\\bottomrule\n442\t\\end{tabular}\n443\t\\end{table}\n444\t\n445\tPathway~A (CIVO): entropy \\emph{decreases} as the actor over-commits to value-overestimated trajectories.\n446\tPathway~B: entropy \\emph{increases} as the policy destabilizes, producing near-random behavior and eval crash.\n447\tThis qualitative signature provides independent diagnostic evidence for the dual-pathway mechanism.\n448\t\n449\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n450\t\\subsection{Value Prediction Bias Distribution}\n451\t\\label{sec:value_dist}\n452\t\n453\tDirect analysis of $V(s)$ vs.\\ Monte Carlo returns reveals:\n454\t\\begin{itemize}\n455\t    \\item \\textbf{008d (trained)}: +45\\% systematic overestimation at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states, right-skewed distribution (heavy positive tail).\n456\t    \\item \\textbf{008f (no dt\\_emb)}: $\\pm$15\\% unbiased noise, symmetric wide distribution.\n457\t    \\item \\textbf{Baseline}: +5\\% mild optimism (normal for DreamerV3).\n458\t\\end{itemize}\n459\t\n460\tThe systematic positive skew exclusively at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ states under trained dt-embedding constitutes direct evidence that CIVO operates through the dt-conditioning channel.\n461\t\n462\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n463\t\\subsection{dt-Embedding Vector Analysis}\n464\t\\label{sec:emb_analysis}\n465\t\n466\t\\begin{table}[h]\n467\t\\centering\n468\t\\caption{dt-embedding vector statistics over training. Trained embeddings develop high norm (``meaningful'') with low cosine similarity (``differentiated'').}\n469\t\\label{tab:emb_vectors}\n470\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n471\t\\toprule\n472\tConfig & Step & $\\|e_{\\mathrm{dt}=1}\\|$ & $\\|e_{\\mathrm{dt}=2}\\|$ & $\\cos(e_1, e_2)$ \\\\\n473\t\\midrule\n474\t008d & 5K & 0.5 & 0.5 & 0.02 \\\\\n475\t008d & 35K & 1.8 & 2.3 & 0.15 \\\\\n476\t008d & 55K & 2.5 & 3.1 & 0.08 \\\\\n477\t008a & 105K & 0.5 & 0.4 & 0.01 \\\\\n478\t015b (frozen) & 105K & 0.5 & 0.5 & 0.00 \\\\\n479\t\\bottomrule\n480\t\\end{tabular}\n481\t\\end{table}\n482\t\n483\tThe trained embedding (008d) develops high norm with low cosine similarity---both $\\mathrm{dt}$ values learn distinct, meaningful representations.\n484\tSince 93\\% of the gradient comes from observation decoders, the embedding encodes dt-specific \\emph{observation reconstruction} patterns, which is precisely the information channel that creates systematic reward prediction bias.\n485\t\n486\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n487\t\\subsection{NVE Threshold Sensitivity and Statistical Analysis}\n488\t\\label{sec:threshold}\n489\t\n490\t\\begin{table}[h]\n491\t\\centering\n492\t\\caption{NVE threshold sensitivity analysis ($n=12$ datapoints at time of analysis, extended to 18 with Cartpole). $1.5\\times$ achieves perfect F1.}\n493\t\\label{tab:threshold}\n494\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n495\t\\toprule\n496\tThreshold & TP & FN & TN & FP & Accuracy & F1 \\\\\n497\t\\midrule\n498\t1.3$\\times$ & 6/6 & 0/6 & 5/6 & 1/6 & 0.92 & 0.92 \\\\\n499\t\\textbf{1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{6/6} & \\textbf{0/6} & \\textbf{6/6} & \\textbf{0/6} & \\textbf{1.00} & \\textbf{1.00} \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VVjg8XcxFS9zdzgBQKsc21","session_id":"a884cd18-9ddc-4e56-828b-637c4a5e9c11","uuid":"78e0a9f0-04de-4b7a-be3c-f75e0a9db68b","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:57:04.000Z"}
